1
00:01:10,420 --> 00:01:13,220
Thank you.

2
00:01:53,720 --> 00:01:56,320
Those fools braved Aqua
Laguna to get here.

3
00:01:56,460 --> 00:01:58,578
They're determined, if nothing else.

4
00:01:59,020 --> 00:02:01,020
Either that or they're just lucky.

5
00:02:01,300 --> 00:02:03,737
Remember, we have permission
to kill this time.

6
00:02:03,820 --> 00:02:07,232
But if we kill them, it'll be
over before we have any fun.

7
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:09,000
The drawbridge? Yeah.

8
00:02:09,320 --> 00:02:12,317
The Franky family's trying to
get that under control right now.

9
00:02:12,400 --> 00:02:16,797
Once they bring it down, we should be
able to get to the other side, no problem.

10
00:02:16,880 --> 00:02:17,710
What a pain.

11
00:02:17,940 --> 00:02:20,528
She's right there and
all we can do is wait.

12
00:02:30,560 --> 00:02:31,390
Damn it.

13
00:02:31,473 --> 00:02:32,580
What is her deal?

14
00:02:32,940 --> 00:02:35,293
She must be hiding one hell of a secret.

15
00:02:37,340 --> 00:02:38,920
Hey, Cape!

16
00:02:39,240 --> 00:02:40,377
Here are your orders.

17
00:02:40,460 --> 00:02:44,342
Kill the intruders, but only engage
them here at the Tower of Law!

18
00:02:45,500 --> 00:02:49,324
My life and my promotion are the
most important things right now.

19
00:02:49,540 --> 00:02:51,217
I have to be careful about this.

20
00:02:51,300 --> 00:02:53,157
If I let all my underlings run off,

21
00:02:53,240 --> 00:02:56,181
who's going to protect me
if something goes wrong?

22
00:02:58,620 --> 00:02:59,450
Ah!

23
00:02:59,533 --> 00:03:00,363
Stupid pirates!

24
00:03:00,580 --> 00:03:04,497
You may act tough, but you don't know
what you're getting yourselves into!

25
00:03:04,580 --> 00:03:07,060
Open your eyes! Come on!

26
00:03:07,320 --> 00:03:10,580
You don't think you can
beat all of CP9, do you?

27
00:03:11,040 --> 00:03:13,158
And what about the Gates of Justice?

28
00:03:13,380 --> 00:03:15,851
You don't think you can open them, do you?

29
00:03:16,120 --> 00:03:19,600
Even then, if things
go wrong, I have this!

30
00:03:19,880 --> 00:03:25,220
The Golden Transponder Snail, giving me
the authority to wish you a Buster Call!

31
00:03:25,720 --> 00:03:26,880
A Buster Call?

32
00:03:27,780 --> 00:03:29,500
Yes, that's right.

33
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:32,380
I'm sure you remember its power well.

34
00:03:32,920 --> 00:03:37,180
After all, it did destroy
your homeland 20 years ago.

35
00:03:37,740 --> 00:03:39,480
What was it, Ohara?

36
00:03:39,800 --> 00:03:43,035
Hard to recall the name since
it was wiped off the map.

37
00:03:43,580 --> 00:03:44,680
Robin's homeland?

38
00:03:45,320 --> 00:03:47,200
He's pissing me off.

39
00:03:47,800 --> 00:03:49,360
There's no place left.

40
00:03:49,620 --> 00:03:51,720
Nowhere for me to go or return to.

41
00:03:53,140 --> 00:03:54,460
Anything but that!

42
00:03:54,800 --> 00:03:56,220
I'm begging you!

43
00:03:57,320 --> 00:04:00,440
Ooh, begging for mercy, I like that.

44
00:04:00,760 --> 00:04:01,720
What's wrong?

45
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:04,197
You don't want me to
press this and annihilate

46
00:04:04,280 --> 00:04:06,417
all your little friends
with a Buster Call?

47
00:04:06,500 --> 00:04:08,088
Is that what you're saying?

48
00:04:09,500 --> 00:04:10,620
You're insane.

49
00:04:10,880 --> 00:04:14,357
If you push that button, do you
have any clue what will happen?

50
00:04:14,440 --> 00:04:15,800
Of course I do.

51
00:04:16,240 --> 00:04:18,217
This button will eliminate any chance your

52
00:04:18,300 --> 00:04:21,297
friends possibly have of
leaving this island alive.

53
00:04:21,380 --> 00:04:23,321
All it takes is one little press.

54
00:04:23,480 --> 00:04:24,757
It couldn't be more simple.

55
00:04:24,840 --> 00:04:27,737
If you think that you
don't understand a thing

56
00:04:27,820 --> 00:04:30,702
are all those old memories
coming back to you now

57
00:04:31,840 --> 00:04:32,780
Let's see.

58
00:04:32,900 --> 00:04:35,080
Maybe I should press it after all.

59
00:04:35,340 --> 00:04:37,040
It might be fun

60
00:04:45,880 --> 00:04:49,060
I'm the one who makes
the demands here, wench?

61
00:05:05,820 --> 00:05:08,600
You say Ohara was wiped off the map,

62
00:05:08,960 --> 00:05:13,017
but your map doesn't show you the
lives of the people who lived there.

63
00:05:13,100 --> 00:05:16,457
Your view of the world
is so distant and so

64
00:05:16,540 --> 00:05:19,780
twisted that you've lost
sight of your humanity.

65
00:05:29,400 --> 00:05:33,576
There's a limit to how much you could
sacrifice in the name of justice.

66
00:05:34,180 --> 00:05:37,140
A Buster Call is going too far.

67
00:05:37,940 --> 00:05:41,300
You can't use it to anything but that.

68
00:05:51,920 --> 00:05:53,517
All right, get her!

69
00:05:53,600 --> 00:05:55,200
Nice throw! Wow!

70
00:05:58,880 --> 00:05:59,717
Yeah!

71
00:05:59,800 --> 00:06:00,720
Bullseye!

72
00:06:00,960 --> 00:06:02,700
Take that, Monster Girls!

73
00:06:03,040 --> 00:06:04,280
You're so creepy!

74
00:06:07,440 --> 00:06:09,660
Monster! Monster! Monster! Monster!

75
00:06:14,340 --> 00:06:17,040
The Monster Girls hit me!

76
00:06:19,760 --> 00:06:21,720
Yeah, I'll be sorry!

77
00:06:44,700 --> 00:06:45,860
Hey, you!

78
00:06:49,640 --> 00:06:52,817
My kids came home crying because
you hit them on the head.

79
00:06:52,900 --> 00:06:54,677
What are you thinking, acting like that?

80
00:06:54,760 --> 00:06:56,936
You stay away from them, you hear me?

81
00:06:58,360 --> 00:07:00,948
Don't you have anything
to say for yourself?

82
00:07:02,080 --> 00:07:04,668
You really are a little
monster, aren't you?

83
00:07:05,980 --> 00:07:08,000
Hey, you come back here.

84
00:07:08,300 --> 00:07:10,300
I'm not done talking with you yet.

85
00:07:17,860 --> 00:07:20,777
Mommy, Mommy, what are we
having for dinner tonight?

86
00:07:20,860 --> 00:07:22,389
Well, what would you like?

87
00:07:23,900 --> 00:07:25,660
Um, um, rice omelette!

88
00:07:27,080 --> 00:07:29,420
You sure like rice omelette, huh? Yep.

89
00:07:29,820 --> 00:07:32,397
Then we'll pick up some
eggs on the way home, okay?

90
00:07:32,480 --> 00:07:33,310
Okay!

91
00:07:42,320 --> 00:07:43,880
Sorry I'm home so late.

92
00:07:44,240 --> 00:07:45,900
Hello, is anyone there?

93
00:07:47,500 --> 00:07:48,460
Aunt Roji?

94
00:08:00,260 --> 00:08:01,757
To Robin, today...

95
00:08:01,840 --> 00:08:04,537
As you must know, is my
little girl's birthday.

96
00:08:04,620 --> 00:08:07,817
So the family is going out
for a special dinner tonight.

97
00:08:07,900 --> 00:08:09,135
Just the three of us.

98
00:08:09,500 --> 00:08:13,500
We left some bread out on the table,
so you can eat that for dinner.

99
00:08:13,840 --> 00:08:15,980
Don't be too greedy with the jam.

100
00:08:17,240 --> 00:08:22,280
When you're done eating, and before we
come back, I want you to do the dishes,

101
00:08:22,700 --> 00:08:23,700
scrub the floors,

102
00:08:24,020 --> 00:08:25,432
take the laundry inside,

103
00:08:25,520 --> 00:08:29,461
and go straight to bed as soon as
you're finished with your chores.

104
00:08:35,760 --> 00:08:38,497
Mommy, Mommy, what are we
having for dinner tonight?

105
00:08:38,580 --> 00:08:40,109
Well, what would you like?

106
00:08:41,480 --> 00:08:43,720
Um, um, rice omelet!

107
00:08:45,440 --> 00:08:47,780
You sure like rice omelettes, huh? Yep!

108
00:08:48,260 --> 00:08:50,477
Then we'll pick up some
eggs on the way home, okay?

109
00:08:50,560 --> 00:08:51,390
Okay!

110
00:09:11,900 --> 00:09:14,100
Oh look, here comes Robin.

111
00:09:14,380 --> 00:09:16,792
Seems she's stopping by to return a book.

112
00:09:17,040 --> 00:09:18,746
Everyone, get in place quick!

113
00:09:23,960 --> 00:09:24,790
Hello?

114
00:09:24,873 --> 00:09:25,703
Professor Clover?

115
00:09:25,880 --> 00:09:27,762
I finished the book you lent me.

116
00:09:32,380 --> 00:09:33,580
One, two...

117
00:09:34,460 --> 00:09:36,060
Congratulations, Robin!

118
00:09:37,280 --> 00:09:38,800
You're amazing, Robin.

119
00:09:38,900 --> 00:09:40,020
A real genius.

120
00:09:40,400 --> 00:09:42,576
We've got a cake for you, by the way.

121
00:09:43,440 --> 00:09:47,937
Robin, that doctoral exam you took the
other day, you passed with a perfect score.

122
00:09:48,020 --> 00:09:52,240
Or, from this day forward, you
are an official archeologist.

123
00:09:52,500 --> 00:09:56,794
And since you're one of us now, we
can all work on our research together,

124
00:09:56,880 --> 00:09:57,762
starting today.

125
00:09:58,840 --> 00:10:01,360
Here, this proves that you're a scholar.

126
00:10:01,740 --> 00:10:02,960
Wear it with pride.

127
00:10:03,380 --> 00:10:05,086
It looks great on you, Robin.

128
00:10:05,280 --> 00:10:06,520
We're so proud.

129
00:10:08,820 --> 00:10:09,940
Now listen up.

130
00:10:10,160 --> 00:10:13,797
You must understand what it means
to be an Ohara archeologist.

131
00:10:13,880 --> 00:10:15,857
Right, I'm ready to learn.

132
00:10:15,940 --> 00:10:17,200
Our knowledge,

133
00:10:17,600 --> 00:10:21,580
in particular, comes from
the past And this library,

134
00:10:21,900 --> 00:10:25,797
known as the Tree of Knowledge
Has stood for over 5,000 years

135
00:10:25,880 --> 00:10:29,037
Collecting countless documents
and literature from every corner

136
00:10:29,120 --> 00:10:33,157
of the world This is a treasure
for all humanity to share and

137
00:10:33,240 --> 00:10:37,660
nourish for future generations
As the world's largest library,

138
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:41,880
knowledge is our pride and joy
Under the branches of this tree,

139
00:10:42,140 --> 00:10:45,157
the finest archaeologists
from every sea have gathered

140
00:10:45,240 --> 00:10:48,520
With this vast wealth of
information at our disposal,

141
00:10:48,860 --> 00:10:51,978
there is no historical
mystery that cannot be solved.

142
00:10:52,880 --> 00:10:55,680
Are you ready to join us? Yes, sir.

143
00:10:56,380 --> 00:10:57,340
In that case,

144
00:10:57,460 --> 00:11:00,257
I look forward to seeing
your research take form and

145
00:11:00,340 --> 00:11:03,437
watching all the contributions
you will make to the world.

146
00:11:03,520 --> 00:11:08,344
Professor, what I'd really like is to find
out what happened in the blank century.

147
00:11:08,680 --> 00:11:10,200
No, you can't!

148
00:11:10,320 --> 00:11:13,280
I strictly forbid it just
as I always have! Huh?

149
00:11:13,460 --> 00:11:14,317
I don't get it.

150
00:11:14,400 --> 00:11:16,717
If we want to solve the
mysteries of the world,

151
00:11:16,800 --> 00:11:20,737
shouldn't we study the Poneglyphs
to find out what happened back then?

152
00:11:20,820 --> 00:11:21,650
What?

153
00:11:21,733 --> 00:11:23,140
How do you know about all that?

154
00:11:23,380 --> 00:11:26,556
Did you use your powers to
peek on us in the basement?

155
00:11:27,060 --> 00:11:27,937
I just...

156
00:11:28,020 --> 00:11:30,677
Any attempt to decipher
or study the Poneglyphs is

157
00:11:30,760 --> 00:11:33,701
considered a criminal act
by the world government.

158
00:11:35,060 --> 00:11:35,920
I see.

159
00:11:36,760 --> 00:11:38,897
So that's why you don't tell anybody.

160
00:11:38,980 --> 00:11:41,980
That's why you study them
in the basement at night.

161
00:11:42,780 --> 00:11:44,640
You were spying on us?

162
00:11:45,200 --> 00:11:46,857
So how much do you know?

163
00:11:46,940 --> 00:11:50,116
This is a very serious matter
we're dealing with here.

164
00:11:50,500 --> 00:11:51,912
Explain yourself, Robin!

165
00:11:53,300 --> 00:11:54,560
I know it's bad.

166
00:11:55,820 --> 00:11:59,526
But even if I asked nicely, You
still wouldn't let me join you.

167
00:12:00,700 --> 00:12:03,994
That's why I studied so hard
to become an archaeologist.

168
00:12:04,340 --> 00:12:08,297
Because I thought then you would
finally treat me like everyone else.

169
00:12:08,380 --> 00:12:10,080
I just wanted to learn.

170
00:12:12,740 --> 00:12:13,570
Robin.

171
00:12:15,940 --> 00:12:17,617
I know this is hard.

172
00:12:17,700 --> 00:12:19,277
You've studied with all your heart.

173
00:12:19,360 --> 00:12:22,857
And deserve to be called a scholar
as much as anyone else here.

174
00:12:22,940 --> 00:12:25,397
However, you're also still a child.

175
00:12:25,480 --> 00:12:29,377
If word gets out, Then every
one of us could be put to death.

176
00:12:29,460 --> 00:12:32,297
Harsh as that sounds, It's
something we're willing to accept.

177
00:12:32,380 --> 00:12:35,937
Since this became the law
of the world 800 years ago,

178
00:12:36,020 --> 00:12:38,197
Countless scholars have lost their lives.

179
00:12:38,280 --> 00:12:40,720
as many as there are stars in the sky.

180
00:12:41,220 --> 00:12:44,220
But we can't stop now,
and I'll tell you why.

181
00:12:44,560 --> 00:12:47,897
Of all the scholars who ever tried
to decipher the ancient texts,

182
00:12:47,980 --> 00:12:51,509
we of Ohara are the only ones
who have met with any success.

183
00:12:52,140 --> 00:12:54,700
For us, there is no turning back.

184
00:12:55,040 --> 00:12:59,687
I swear on the Tree of Knowledge, if you
ever come close to the basement again,

185
00:12:59,960 --> 00:13:02,157
you will be forbidden
from entering the library?

186
00:13:02,240 --> 00:13:04,057
or conducting research with us.

187
00:13:04,140 --> 00:13:05,022
You understand?

188
00:13:09,540 --> 00:13:10,370
Robin!

189
00:13:11,020 --> 00:13:15,080
I hate to say this, especially
now, but she's just like her mom.

190
00:13:15,380 --> 00:13:19,145
It's only a matter of time before
she uncovers that secret, too.

191
00:13:19,600 --> 00:13:23,720
Her mother, Olvia, was only
captured by Marines a few days ago.

192
00:13:24,040 --> 00:13:26,900
But it's already clear
what fate she'll suffer.

193
00:13:57,440 --> 00:14:01,280
Oh, my God.

194
00:14:16,120 --> 00:14:18,280
Oh, water.

195
00:14:20,820 --> 00:14:22,220
Water?

196
00:14:23,180 --> 00:14:24,120
Water?

197
00:14:31,800 --> 00:14:33,200
Perfect.

198
00:14:42,200 --> 00:14:44,580
Guess I'm still alive after all.

199
00:14:45,280 --> 00:14:48,520
I just wonder if she's okay, too.

200
00:14:50,760 --> 00:14:52,760
Anyhow, thank you for your help.

201
00:14:52,940 --> 00:14:58,140
But I gotta ask, is there a town or
village somewhere around here? Mm-hmm.

202
00:14:58,860 --> 00:14:59,742
That's a shame.

203
00:15:01,780 --> 00:15:04,220
I suppose bakers can't be choosers,

204
00:15:04,460 --> 00:15:07,578
but a deserted island would
have been nice, you know?

205
00:15:14,300 --> 00:15:19,377
you can stare all you want but ain't
nothing gonna happen as you can see

206
00:15:19,460 --> 00:15:24,957
I hurt my legs so I'm staying put
least for the time being after I feel

207
00:15:25,040 --> 00:15:28,197
a little better I'll build a raft
or something to get me out of here

208
00:15:28,280 --> 00:15:32,177
this your first time seeing a giant

209
00:15:32,260 --> 00:15:36,477
missy yeah I'll tell you one thing I

210
00:15:36,560 --> 00:15:40,717
may be as big as those guys from
albath but i sure ain't as mean just

211
00:15:40,800 --> 00:15:47,677
as sure as there are warlike giants
there's peaceful ones too i know this

212
00:15:47,760 --> 00:15:52,197
is a long shot but i gotta ask anyway
when you get home you think maybe

213
00:15:52,280 --> 00:15:57,280
you could uh not tell anyone that a
giant washed up on the beach okay sure

214
00:15:58,180 --> 00:16:04,040
well that was too easy of course you're
gonna tell people i said i wouldn't

215
00:16:07,880 --> 00:16:09,700
Yeah, you tellin' a fib.

216
00:16:09,900 --> 00:16:13,140
Ain't no way a kid can
keep a secret this big.

217
00:16:13,760 --> 00:16:15,220
Don't worry about it.

218
00:16:15,360 --> 00:16:17,301
I'm not really interested in you.

219
00:16:33,960 --> 00:16:34,790
Derishi?

220
00:16:34,873 --> 00:16:36,100
What does that mean?

221
00:16:36,940 --> 00:16:38,477
It don't mean a darn thing.

222
00:16:38,560 --> 00:16:39,619
I'm just laughing.

223
00:16:42,220 --> 00:16:43,760
That's the way you laugh?

224
00:16:44,280 --> 00:16:46,520
You sure are a funny little girl.

225
00:16:51,700 --> 00:16:53,160
You're the funny one.

226
00:16:56,100 --> 00:17:00,580
I gotta admit, all my life people have
been telling me my laugh is silly.

227
00:17:00,800 --> 00:17:03,880
But you, you're pretty
cute when you laugh.

228
00:17:04,180 --> 00:17:05,717
You should do it more often.

229
00:17:05,800 --> 00:17:06,630
And I say,

230
00:17:09,180 --> 00:17:11,220
Anyhow, I go by the name Saul.

231
00:17:11,740 --> 00:17:12,570
Jaguar D.

232
00:17:12,760 --> 00:17:14,280
Saul, if you like.

233
00:17:15,060 --> 00:17:15,890
D?

234
00:17:16,400 --> 00:17:19,217
Yeah, I don't much know what it means.

235
00:17:19,300 --> 00:17:23,277
But from what I hear, everyone in my
family has it for their middle name.

236
00:17:23,360 --> 00:17:24,772
Anyways, what about you?

237
00:17:25,080 --> 00:17:26,900
My name's Robin. I see.

238
00:17:27,860 --> 00:17:31,537
Well, truth is, Robin, some guys
are chasing me down right now.

239
00:17:31,620 --> 00:17:34,517
So, uh, if someone finds
me, I'm in trouble.

240
00:17:34,600 --> 00:17:36,200
I said, I wouldn't tell!

241
00:17:47,640 --> 00:17:48,470
Good.

242
00:17:48,940 --> 00:17:50,200
Here's another one.

243
00:17:53,320 --> 00:17:57,060
Mighty kind to you, Robin, but
you go ahead and take this one.

244
00:17:57,400 --> 00:17:59,780
My stomach's pretty full as is.

245
00:18:00,120 --> 00:18:03,260
As you can see, I caught
a real Wapol last night.

246
00:18:05,780 --> 00:18:08,880
Well, I better get back
to work on that raft.

247
00:18:08,980 --> 00:18:11,509
It should be done pretty
soon at this rate.

248
00:18:16,040 --> 00:18:20,757
I don't mean to pry, but it seems like you
have an awful lot of time on your hands.

249
00:18:20,840 --> 00:18:22,577
Not that I don't like your company.

250
00:18:22,660 --> 00:18:24,895
You've been sticking with me for days.

251
00:18:25,040 --> 00:18:28,057
Ain't your parents and friends
going to get suspicious?

252
00:18:28,140 --> 00:18:29,740
I don't have any parents.

253
00:18:30,440 --> 00:18:34,797
Actually, I do have a mom, but she set sail
for work years ago and never came back.

254
00:18:34,880 --> 00:18:36,597
I don't even remember her face.

255
00:18:36,680 --> 00:18:39,337
I'm sort of friends with the
archaeologists around here,

256
00:18:39,420 --> 00:18:43,397
but they won't let me join in on their
research, so I don't know anymore.

257
00:18:43,480 --> 00:18:47,217
The other kids say I'm creepy, so
even if I wanted to play with them,

258
00:18:47,300 --> 00:18:49,241
I don't think that they'd let me.

259
00:18:51,000 --> 00:18:53,980
Ah, so you have Devil Fruit powers, huh?

260
00:18:54,920 --> 00:18:56,332
You aren't scared of me?

261
00:18:56,460 --> 00:18:59,657
I used to sail all over the
Grand Line back in the day.

262
00:18:59,740 --> 00:19:03,257
I've seen plenty of folks with
powers ton scarier than yours.

263
00:19:03,340 --> 00:19:06,752
To be honest, I'm kind of
jealous of you little arm thing.

264
00:19:07,100 --> 00:19:09,900
It looks pretty handy. Daddy!

265
00:19:11,800 --> 00:19:12,630
Daddy!

266
00:19:16,040 --> 00:19:18,680
That laugh of yours is
really weird. Daddy!

267
00:19:19,500 --> 00:19:21,735
It don't matter if your laugh's weird.

268
00:19:21,920 --> 00:19:26,802
All that really matters is when you laugh,
all of a sudden your problems disappear.

269
00:19:27,120 --> 00:19:27,950
Why?

270
00:19:28,033 --> 00:19:29,157
What do you mean, why?

271
00:19:29,240 --> 00:19:32,475
Everyone knows you laugh when
you're having fun, right?

272
00:19:32,720 --> 00:19:35,497
So if you're not having fun,
all you need to do is laugh,

273
00:19:35,580 --> 00:19:37,777
and everything will work out just fine.

274
00:19:37,860 --> 00:19:38,960
You're just a kid.

275
00:19:39,220 --> 00:19:41,337
But it seems like you're
having a tough time.

276
00:19:41,420 --> 00:19:43,257
When you're feeling down, just laugh.

277
00:19:43,340 --> 00:19:45,717
There ain't nothing a
good laugh can't fix.

278
00:19:45,800 --> 00:19:48,624
It seems awfully silly
to laugh when you're sad.

279
00:19:48,760 --> 00:19:51,680
Don't knock until you try it. Here. Dad!

280
00:19:53,200 --> 00:19:57,024
Do it just like that, and I'll
bet you'll feel better in a flash.

281
00:20:03,480 --> 00:20:04,477
Not bad!

282
00:20:04,560 --> 00:20:05,880
Now, with more gusto!

283
00:20:14,160 --> 00:20:16,140
Now that's more like it!

284
00:20:20,880 --> 00:20:23,100
This is Navy Headquarters here.

285
00:20:23,320 --> 00:20:25,997
Once again, we're hoping we
can speak to Chief Spandine.

286
00:20:26,080 --> 00:20:27,197
You heard him, Chief.

287
00:20:27,280 --> 00:20:28,660
What now? Ignore them.

288
00:20:28,920 --> 00:20:31,273
They probably just want a status report.

289
00:20:31,380 --> 00:20:33,417
We'll contact them when we see the shore.

290
00:20:33,500 --> 00:20:34,618
That's good enough.

291
00:20:35,140 --> 00:20:37,640
Ohara, opera, oh whatever, who cares?

292
00:20:38,000 --> 00:20:41,637
Why are they wasting my time making me
trek all the way out here to the West Blue,

293
00:20:41,720 --> 00:20:42,550
huh?

294
00:20:42,633 --> 00:20:45,808
Let's get this over with, then
get the hell out of this dump.

295
00:21:07,060 --> 00:21:09,877
Hey, is it true we've got
a Navy ship on the island?

296
00:21:09,960 --> 00:21:10,790
What?

297
00:21:10,873 --> 00:21:11,917
I haven't heard about that.

298
00:21:12,000 --> 00:21:13,717
Yeah, it must have
anchored here overnight.

299
00:21:13,800 --> 00:21:15,277
Why would the Navy come here?

300
00:21:15,360 --> 00:21:16,190
I know.

301
00:21:16,273 --> 00:21:17,296
We're pretty remote.

302
00:21:17,540 --> 00:21:19,517
Weird thing is, when people
went to look at the boat,

303
00:21:19,600 --> 00:21:21,237
they didn't see any Marines there.

304
00:21:21,320 --> 00:21:22,520
Wow, that is weird.

305
00:21:22,700 --> 00:21:25,737
It was like a ghost ship, not
a single person to be found.

306
00:21:25,820 --> 00:21:28,232
Come on, now you're just fooling with us.

307
00:21:28,560 --> 00:21:29,390
Professor!

308
00:21:29,500 --> 00:21:30,720
Professor Clover!

309
00:21:33,960 --> 00:21:35,960
My goodness, did something happen?

310
00:21:36,220 --> 00:21:38,117
I hope it's nothing to worry about,

311
00:21:38,200 --> 00:21:41,557
but I just heard that there's a huge
Navy ship anchored at the seashore.

312
00:21:41,640 --> 00:21:42,757
We should take precautions.

313
00:21:42,840 --> 00:21:44,546
It might be an investigation.

314
00:21:45,740 --> 00:21:47,799
Better let the other scholars know.

315
00:21:50,380 --> 00:21:53,080
The raft is done! All right!

316
00:21:58,720 --> 00:22:00,249
So you're leaving already?

317
00:22:00,880 --> 00:22:02,700
Now what's with that face?

318
00:22:02,980 --> 00:22:05,804
You put those puppy dog
eyes away, little missy.

319
00:22:07,920 --> 00:22:11,060
Actually, I suppose my
raft could use a flag, huh?

320
00:22:11,280 --> 00:22:14,162
Guess I'll just have to
stay a little bit longer.

321
00:22:21,100 --> 00:22:22,520
I'll be darned.

322
00:22:22,660 --> 00:22:25,307
So you're looking to
set out to sea too, huh?

323
00:22:26,480 --> 00:22:28,637
My mom's been away from home for years

324
00:22:28,720 --> 00:22:31,197
thanks to all her big
archaeological research.

325
00:22:31,280 --> 00:22:33,977
But whenever she finally
comes back to the island,

326
00:22:34,060 --> 00:22:37,077
I'm going to ask her to take
me along on her next trip.

327
00:22:37,160 --> 00:22:39,337
That's why I've been studying so hard.

328
00:22:39,420 --> 00:22:42,037
I got my archaeology
doctorate just like her.

329
00:22:42,120 --> 00:22:43,620
You got a doctorate?

330
00:22:43,960 --> 00:22:47,000
A tiny little girly like you? Mm-hmm.

331
00:22:47,960 --> 00:22:49,180
But hold on a sec.

332
00:22:49,780 --> 00:22:53,417
Didn't you tell me you don't even
remember what your mom's face looks like?

333
00:22:53,500 --> 00:22:56,120
Yeah, but she's still my mom.

334
00:22:56,740 --> 00:22:58,260
I want to see her.

335
00:23:01,560 --> 00:23:02,600
Hey, Sol.

336
00:23:03,260 --> 00:23:05,597
Did you know that our
world has a period of

337
00:23:05,680 --> 00:23:08,760
100 years that nobody knows
anything about? Uh-huh.

338
00:23:09,020 --> 00:23:12,314
I believe folks call that the
blank century, don't they?

339
00:23:12,460 --> 00:23:13,897
I'm curious about it myself,

340
00:23:13,980 --> 00:23:17,177
but the government says it's
illegal for anyone to look into.

341
00:23:17,260 --> 00:23:18,090
That's right.

342
00:23:18,220 --> 00:23:22,808
But I heard my mom is traveling all over
the world investigating it in secret.

343
00:23:24,180 --> 00:23:25,010
it.

344
00:23:25,093 --> 00:23:25,923
But don't tell anyone.

345
00:23:26,006 --> 00:23:27,197
I don't want her to get in trouble.

346
00:23:27,280 --> 00:23:28,110
What?

347
00:23:28,193 --> 00:23:30,277
Are you telling me that your
mom's going around looking

348
00:23:30,360 --> 00:23:33,772
for them ancient stone tablet
things they call poneglyphs?

349
00:23:33,940 --> 00:23:35,720
You've heard about poneglyphs?

350
00:23:35,840 --> 00:23:37,897
Yeah, but that ain't the point here, okay?

351
00:23:37,980 --> 00:23:40,037
You can't go around
talking about this kind of

352
00:23:40,120 --> 00:23:42,197
stuff in front of other
folks out of the blue.

353
00:23:42,280 --> 00:23:46,751
And if your mama's out there looking
for them, You especially gotta be good.

354
00:23:48,080 --> 00:23:48,910
Huh?

355
00:23:48,993 --> 00:23:49,823
Is something wrong?

356
00:23:50,020 --> 00:23:53,680
Robin, do you happen to
know your mother's name?

357
00:23:54,080 --> 00:23:55,140
It's Olvia.

358
00:23:57,120 --> 00:23:58,600
Nico Olvia.

359
00:24:06,640 --> 00:24:07,470
You okay?

360
00:24:08,080 --> 00:24:10,960
Robin is Olvia's daughter.

361
00:24:12,740 --> 00:24:17,360
Wait, so does that mean I've
been on O 'Hara all this time?

362
00:24:17,540 --> 00:24:18,370
Mm-hmm

363
00:24:18,600 --> 00:24:20,420
Oh, that's terrible!

364
00:24:21,060 --> 00:24:21,890
Oh, no!

365
00:24:22,120 --> 00:24:25,220
Why did I end up on O 'Hara of all places?

366
00:24:26,400 --> 00:24:28,577
This is no time to be
sitting around, Saul?

367
00:24:28,660 --> 00:24:30,140
Robin Yes?

368
00:24:30,580 --> 00:24:31,797
This may come as a shock,

369
00:24:31,880 --> 00:24:35,557
but you gotta listen to me and
listen good Navy battleships are on

370
00:24:35,640 --> 00:24:40,820
their way to O 'Hara right now They
may even be here already The Navy? Why?

371
00:24:40,960 --> 00:24:44,431
They're going to wipe out every
last scholar on the island.

372
00:24:45,520 --> 00:24:47,120
That can't be. No.

373
00:24:48,380 --> 00:24:51,300
So listen, whatever
you do... You're lying.

374
00:24:51,520 --> 00:24:54,077
You can't let those government
guys know you're a scholar.

375
00:24:54,160 --> 00:24:54,990
You're lying.

376
00:24:55,073 --> 00:24:56,257
You've got to promise me that, okay?

377
00:24:56,340 --> 00:24:57,620
Stop lying to me!

378
00:24:57,800 --> 00:24:59,137
It's true, I swear!

379
00:24:59,220 --> 00:25:01,697
I wouldn't joke about
something this serious!

380
00:25:01,780 --> 00:25:05,377
Get to town as quick as you can and
see if anything strange is going on.

381
00:25:05,460 --> 00:25:08,754
I know it sounds crazy, but
your mom might be there too.

382
00:25:16,820 --> 00:25:21,000
It's been a while, hasn't
it? It's Olya. You came back.

383
00:25:21,140 --> 00:25:23,357
We heard news that you've
been captured by the Navy.

384
00:25:23,440 --> 00:25:25,793
Don't tell me I came back on their ship.

385
00:25:32,840 --> 00:25:37,840
I hate to have to tell you this as soon as
I return, but the news you heard is true.

386
00:25:38,140 --> 00:25:39,077
It's not your fault.

387
00:25:39,160 --> 00:25:42,537
We knew the government would set
its sights on Ohara eventually.

388
00:25:42,620 --> 00:25:44,444
I'm not sure how much you know,

389
00:25:44,580 --> 00:25:46,517
but the Poneglyph search team that set off

390
00:25:46,600 --> 00:25:49,247
six years ago was
annihilated by the Marines.

391
00:25:49,440 --> 00:25:51,237
Thirty-three researchers were killed.

392
00:25:51,320 --> 00:25:52,197
I was the...

393
00:25:52,280 --> 00:25:53,840
the only survivor.

394
00:25:54,320 --> 00:25:56,380
Fortunately, I was only captured.

395
00:25:56,640 --> 00:25:59,077
But I overheard the
Marines talking after they

396
00:25:59,160 --> 00:26:01,257
had rummaged through the
search team's belongings.

397
00:26:01,340 --> 00:26:04,140
They soon realized that
we all came from Ohara.

398
00:26:05,280 --> 00:26:07,020
I won't make any excuses.

399
00:26:07,560 --> 00:26:11,600
All of you are in danger because
of my carelessness. Stop it.

400
00:26:11,900 --> 00:26:14,017
Thinking like that won't do any good.

401
00:26:14,100 --> 00:26:18,697
Those who set out to sea and those who
stayed behind are all part of the same team.

402
00:26:18,780 --> 00:26:22,133
After what happened, if anyone
should apologize, it's us.

403
00:26:22,740 --> 00:26:24,681
We know how much you've suffered.

404
00:26:26,540 --> 00:26:27,370
Professor.

405
00:26:27,620 --> 00:26:31,937
For years now, the world government has
seen Ohara as a threat to their rule.

406
00:26:32,020 --> 00:26:34,637
But all we can do is
believe in the truth of

407
00:26:34,720 --> 00:26:37,640
history and its value to
all generations to come.

408
00:26:37,880 --> 00:26:41,157
Be that as it may, they've put us
in quite a compromising position.

409
00:26:41,240 --> 00:26:43,737
I just don't know how we're
going to get out of this one.

410
00:26:43,820 --> 00:26:44,650
I admit,

411
00:26:44,733 --> 00:26:45,657
it doesn't look good,

412
00:26:45,740 --> 00:26:48,757
but we're fortunate to have discovered
this information when we did.

413
00:26:48,840 --> 00:26:52,037
We know that the government is hunting
down all the scholars on the island.

414
00:26:52,120 --> 00:26:56,120
So before they make it to the
library, we have to escape from Ohara.

415
00:26:56,380 --> 00:26:57,380
You have a point.

416
00:26:58,220 --> 00:27:01,937
This time I have a feeling we won't be
able to slip past their investigation.

417
00:27:02,020 --> 00:27:03,196
like we have before.

418
00:27:03,300 --> 00:27:07,117
And if they're committed to killing
us, they won't listen to reason either.

419
00:27:07,200 --> 00:27:08,640
Then hurry up and run!

420
00:27:08,920 --> 00:27:10,120
I'm sorry, Olvia.

421
00:27:10,380 --> 00:27:15,027
I can't let myself escape and leave one
of mankind's greatest treasures behind.

422
00:27:15,260 --> 00:27:16,520
You should know that.

423
00:27:16,880 --> 00:27:19,540
Everyone else here feels
the same way. Yeah.

424
00:27:19,980 --> 00:27:20,860
Are you sure?

425
00:27:21,500 --> 00:27:24,417
We still don't know what the
government's gonna do when they get here.

426
00:27:24,500 --> 00:27:28,297
But we do know if we run, we can't
protect all this precious knowledge.

427
00:27:28,380 --> 00:27:30,217
We're not planning on dying here.

428
00:27:30,300 --> 00:27:31,130
Don't worry.

429
00:27:31,213 --> 00:27:35,900
More importantly, I know you have
something else weighing on your mind.

430
00:27:36,300 --> 00:27:37,300
I can't.

431
00:27:37,520 --> 00:27:38,980
It's too late for that.

432
00:27:39,860 --> 00:27:41,540
Make up your mind, Olvia.

433
00:27:41,860 --> 00:27:44,057
Are you going to board this ship or not?

434
00:27:44,140 --> 00:27:45,877
If you want to stay with your daughter,

435
00:27:45,960 --> 00:27:49,197
we'll settle your husband's
unfinished business for you.

436
00:27:49,280 --> 00:27:51,140
No, I'm not turning back.

437
00:27:51,420 --> 00:27:53,220
I'm a scholar of Ohara.

438
00:27:53,640 --> 00:27:57,740
It's my duty to carry out the
will of those who came before me.

439
00:27:58,400 --> 00:27:59,580
Forgive me, Robin.

440
00:28:00,140 --> 00:28:04,200
I swear, I'll solve these mysteries
and come back to you someday.

441
00:28:09,180 --> 00:28:10,400
Where's my mommy?

442
00:28:11,980 --> 00:28:13,140
Where'd she go?

443
00:28:13,600 --> 00:28:15,917
Your mom had to go out to sea for her job.

444
00:28:16,000 --> 00:28:18,637
You'll be staying here with
us for a little while, okay?

445
00:28:18,720 --> 00:28:19,560
That's enough!

446
00:28:19,740 --> 00:28:21,097
If you don't shut up, I'll hit you!

447
00:28:21,180 --> 00:28:22,057
Honey, stop it.

448
00:28:22,140 --> 00:28:23,022
She's only two.

449
00:28:23,280 --> 00:28:24,633
And so is our daughter.

450
00:28:24,800 --> 00:28:26,957
Listen, I don't care if
she's your sister's child.

451
00:28:27,040 --> 00:28:27,922
She's a burden.

452
00:28:28,080 --> 00:28:29,300
This was a mistake.

453
00:28:34,760 --> 00:28:35,590
Who's there?

454
00:28:35,700 --> 00:28:37,112
What are you doing here?

455
00:28:40,060 --> 00:28:40,937
I was...

456
00:28:41,020 --> 00:28:42,660
I mean, I just...

457
00:28:45,380 --> 00:28:46,700
I'm so sorry!

458
00:28:49,180 --> 00:28:52,617
If you want to read a book, all
you have to do is ask, all right?

459
00:28:52,700 --> 00:28:55,497
But I can't, because I
don't have any money.

460
00:28:55,580 --> 00:29:00,097
Well, then, looks like you're in luck because
you can read everything here for free.

461
00:29:00,180 --> 00:29:01,280
For free? Uh-huh.

462
00:29:01,520 --> 00:29:07,040
After all, this library is the best in the
world. It is? Really? Sure is. Let's see.

463
00:29:07,240 --> 00:29:09,887
Unless I'm mistaken,
you're Olvia's daughter.

464
00:29:10,300 --> 00:29:12,700
You like to read books? Mm-hmm.

465
00:29:14,840 --> 00:29:17,840
She looks just like you
did when you were that age.

466
00:29:18,020 --> 00:29:21,079
And I'm proud to say she's
every bit as clever, too.

467
00:29:21,880 --> 00:29:23,960
If she's safe, that's enough.

468
00:29:25,060 --> 00:29:28,700
When I decided to join you,
I severed all bonds with her.

469
00:29:29,400 --> 00:29:32,341
I can't let her become the
daughter of a criminal.

470
00:29:33,260 --> 00:29:34,090
Bad news!

471
00:29:34,320 --> 00:29:35,420
Another ship came!

472
00:29:35,560 --> 00:29:36,997
With the world government's flag!

473
00:29:37,080 --> 00:29:41,297
One of their high-ranked officials
coming all the way here to the West Blue?

474
00:29:41,380 --> 00:29:42,210
My goodness.

475
00:29:42,293 --> 00:29:44,540
It seems this is quite serious indeed.

476
00:29:46,900 --> 00:29:48,417
All right, everyone, listen.

477
00:29:48,500 --> 00:29:52,265
From now on, you and I are neither
colleagues nor acquaintances.

478
00:29:53,380 --> 00:29:55,717
You have to stick to that no matter what.

479
00:29:55,800 --> 00:29:56,937
Farewell.

480
00:29:57,020 --> 00:30:00,157
Oh wait, what are you going to do?

481
00:30:00,240 --> 00:30:01,157
All right.

482
00:30:01,240 --> 00:30:03,037
Tell HQ we've arrived.

483
00:30:03,120 --> 00:30:05,257
Let's move it.

484
00:30:05,340 --> 00:30:07,046
I want to get this over with.

485
00:30:08,060 --> 00:30:09,217
What a pain.

486
00:30:09,300 --> 00:30:14,757
They better give me a promotion
when this crap is over. Hey.

487
00:30:14,840 --> 00:30:16,217
I want you to hunt down.

488
00:30:16,300 --> 00:30:18,417
on every sculler on the island
and must straighten them.

489
00:30:18,500 --> 00:30:19,330
Yes, sir!

490
00:30:19,580 --> 00:30:20,537
What's going on?

491
00:30:20,620 --> 00:30:22,973
Some woman is running around with a gun!

492
00:30:41,820 --> 00:30:43,737
This is an awful mess.

493
00:30:43,820 --> 00:30:45,960
I'm sorry, Robin!

494
00:30:46,820 --> 00:30:48,020
Vice Admiral Kuzan!

495
00:30:48,740 --> 00:30:49,740
What is it?

496
00:30:49,860 --> 00:30:53,860
We've been informed that CP-9's
Chief Spandine has arrived at Ohara.

497
00:30:54,000 --> 00:30:54,840
So what?

498
00:30:55,140 --> 00:30:57,280
Did you really have to wake me up?

499
00:30:57,520 --> 00:31:00,460
Just to tell me that?! I'm sorry, sir!

500
00:31:15,340 --> 00:31:17,777
I didn't say anything, I was bored.

501
00:31:17,860 --> 00:31:23,960
I was looking for living
while I was living.

502
00:31:25,020 --> 00:31:27,377
I was a single

503
00:31:27,460 --> 00:31:32,437
light that I found.

504
00:31:32,520 --> 00:31:35,520
I felt like I saw.

505
00:31:36,140 --> 00:31:39,477
If you follow me, the
future will run away!

506
00:31:39,560 --> 00:31:44,040
The future will not be settled.

507
00:31:47,560 --> 00:31:51,137
Every time, every time, stop.

508
00:31:51,220 --> 00:31:57,320
The smile and the tears will be filled.

509
00:31:59,560 --> 00:32:04,877
As long as a dark night?
